1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,820
The fake city was real.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:07,820
The proof was in a letter that had been
handled so many times the fold lines

3
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:13,320
had worn through on one side, and the
paper opened there without being told.

4
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:18,940
Not all the way. Paper that old does not
fall open dramatically.

5
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:26,180
It releases. Along the crease where a thumb
had pressed and pressed again over years

6
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:31,180
of returning. The fibre had given way to
something thinner than paper.

7
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:36,360
Thinner than intention. And the letter
understood its own opening.

8
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:42,020
The way a door understands when someone
who has come through it a thousand times is

9
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:46,920
approaching. The fold found its position
before the hand arrived.

10
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:52,640
The person holding it, this matters so
stay with it,

11
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:57,140
was not holding the letter the way you
hold something you are about to read.

12
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:00,420
The body position was wrong for reading.

13
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:04,720
The elbows were bent inward, the forearms
parallel,

14
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:10,520
both hands cupped around the paper as if
the paper were a living thing that might

15
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:12,920
startle and require steadying.

16
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:19,160
This was the grip of someone who had been
surprised once and had not been able

17
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:20,620
to afford surprise again.

18
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:23,780
February 1894.

19
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:27,360
That was the date visible in the upper
left corner,

20
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:30,020
the month and year in faded ink.

21
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:34,420
And below it a name, James Coburn.

22
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:40,740
The ink had the sepia quality of something
written when what we call sepia was simply

23
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:42,720
what ink looked like when it aged.

24
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:48,300
Not a stylistic choice, not a filter
applied to simulate the past,

25
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:51,900
but the past itself, drying out.

26
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:55,940
The water stain in the upper right corner
was oval,

27
00:01:57,020 --> 00:01:59,740
approximately the size of a thumb pressed
sideways.

28
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:03,580
The ink beneath it was blurred but intact.

29
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:08,320
The stain had arrived at an angle that
missed the line of text,

30
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:13,680
blurring only the margin, leaving the
words themselves readable.

31
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,620
The person holding the letter had noticed
this the first time they read it.

32
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:23,360
They had thought about what the oval stain
meant,

33
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:30,700
where the water had come from, where the Coburn
had been caught in weather while writing,

34
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:36,980
or whether the stain had arrived later during
one of the many hands through which this

35
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:41,140
document had travelled between 1894 and
now.

36
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:44,340
They had never resolved this.

37
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:48,320
They had learned to look at the oval the
way you learn to look at a

38
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:53,600
question that has no answer, steadily,
without demanding a resolution.

39
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:57,820
What were the walls of the white city made
of?

40
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:04,960
That is where this investigation begins,
with a letter worn through at the fold lines

41
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:10,960
and a question that sounds simple enough,
and a labourer named James Coburn who found

42
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:14,440
the answer with his hands before he had
language for it.

43
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:18,460
The letter did not start with a
declaration.

44
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:22,360
It started with a practical problem.

45
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:30,440
He wrote, I am writing regarding the work on
the south wing section of the manufacturer's

46
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:36,420
building, and certain things I have found
that I did not expect to find and that

47
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:41,720
are causing me trouble on account of the
plaster not behaving the way the specifications

48
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:43,640
say it should behave.

49
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:48,720
He wrote the way a careful working man
writes when he is building a case,

50
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:56,020
not with flourish, not with drama, but
with the patient accumulation of specific

51
00:03:56,020 --> 00:04:01,580
observation that might someday serve as
documentation in a dispute over billing.

52
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:04,120
He had no interest in mystery.

53
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:06,460
He had an interest in accuracy.

54
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:10,640
These two things are not always separable.

55
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,640
Then toward the middle of the second page,

56
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:20,460
it gave back more than it should have,
like ringing a pan and hearing a bell.

57
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:24,780
The person holding the letter read this
passage one more time,

58
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:32,320
not allowed. The lips moved slightly, the
way lips move when reading something that

59
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:38,760
requires verification, when the mind needs
the body's confirmation that the words are

60
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:45,720
what they appear to be. Then the letter
was folded carefully along

61
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:47,260
the lines it already knew.

62
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:51,220
What were the walls of the white city made
of?

63
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:54,820
May 1st, 1893.

64
00:04:56,580 --> 00:04:58,240
Jackson Park, Chicago.

65
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:03,400
The shore of Lake Michigan, south of the
river.

66
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:07,580
The lake wind had come off the water since
morning,

67
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:10,540
and what it carried was not winter.

68
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,500
Winter had released its hold on the
lakefront by then,

69
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:19,180
but the specific cold that Lake Michigan
produces in early May,

70
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:23,200
the cold of water that has not yet
absorbed the season's warmth,

71
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:27,420
water that is still operating on the
thermal logic of March,

72
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:30,720
even though the calendar has moved past
it.

73
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:34,400
The temperature was cool enough for a
coat,

74
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:40,720
but not cold enough to justify one, which
is the specific meteorological category that

75
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:44,860
makes people stand around in crowds
looking mildly underprepared.

76
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:48,440
There were 20 ,000 people in the Court of
Honor.

77
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:54,220
The official estimates would say somewhat
less than that for the opening ceremony.

78
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:58,920
The formal count didn't include the crowds
held back at the perimeter,

79
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:04,820
waiting for entry, watching the officials
process between the buildings in their frock

80
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:08,780
coats and top hat. But inside the Court of
Honor,

81
00:06:09,460 --> 00:06:12,420
in the open space between the great white
buildings,

82
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:16,460
between the lagoon and the Calanade
facades,

83
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:21,600
and the gilded dome of the administration
building at the center of it all,

84
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,540
20 ,000 people standing in the dusk
waiting for something to happen.

85
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:34,600
The buildings, at that hour, were shapes.

86
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:40,880
The light had gone out of the western sky
about 40 minutes before the ceremony was

87
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:44,220
scheduled to begin. And the Court of Honor
buildings,

88
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:49,180
the agriculture building, the
manufacturers and liberal arts building,

89
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:55,360
the machinery hall, all of them presenting
their neoclassical facades to the lagoon,

90
00:06:55,360 --> 00:07:01,160
had resolved into silhouette, great white
silhouette.

91
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:08,840
The columns and cornices and pediments were
still visible as shapes even after the light

92
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:15,100
was gone, because the calcium sulfate surface
of the staff plaster had a quality that

93
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:20,500
caught whatever residual illumination remained
in the sky and held it slightly longer

94
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:22,280
than ordinary surfaces would.

95
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:26,560
The buildings didn't go dark when the sky
went dark.

96
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:34,900
They faded. And in the fading, they seemed
paradoxically larger.

97
00:07:36,140 --> 00:07:37,780
This was the moment before.

98
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:44,660
The smell in the crowd, wool coats and the
chemical perfume of new paint not yet

99
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:47,540
cured, and from somewhere beyond the
lagoon,

100
00:07:47,980 --> 00:07:49,820
the smell of construction lumber.

101
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:54,260
The white city had been built in
approximately 30 months,

102
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:59,560
and the lumber hadn't had time to fully
integrate with the damp Chicago air.

103
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:05,600
And it still announced itself in gaps between
the decorative surfaces where rain had

104
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:10,640
gotten in and encouraged the wood to release
its own character into the atmosphere.

105
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:13,140
There was also food.

106
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:19,480
The concession stands along the midway
pleasant's had been open since the afternoon,

107
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:25,920
and the smell of fried things moved through
the crowd in gusts whenever the lake wind

108
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:31,180
shifted. And the sound of the crowd, not
silence.

109
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:37,520
The specific sound of 20 ,000 people
simultaneously deciding not to speak.

110
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:40,160
The baseline was still there.

111
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:45,240
Coughs, children, the creek of dress shoes
against flagstone,

112
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:47,840
the distant clatter of the midway.

113
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,020
But the conversational layer had been
withdrawn,

114
00:08:53,020 --> 00:08:56,100
held in reserve for the moment that was
about to arrive.

115
00:08:57,400 --> 00:08:58,900
The buildings were dark.

116
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:04,020
Then President Grover Cleveland pressed
the gilded telegraph key.

117
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:07,480
What happened next was not gradual.

118
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:11,220
That is the detail the photographs cannot
convey,

119
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:16,980
the detail that the account in the official
program hadn't adequately prepared the crowd

120
00:09:16,980 --> 00:09:22,840
for, the detail that separated the memory of
those who were present from the understanding

121
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:25,480
of those who arrived later through images.

122
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:30,180
The Westinghouse Tesla alternating current
system,

123
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:32,880
the complete polyphase system.

124
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:38,480
The first time such a system had been deployed
for general illumination at this scale,

125
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:45,080
didn't warm up. It didn't flicker on from
dim to bright over the course of seconds.

126
00:09:46,100 --> 00:09:50,280
When the circuit closed, the lamps came
alive all at once,

127
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:57,060
immediately, in the manner of something that
had been waiting under pressure for exactly

128
00:09:57,060 --> 00:10:01,020
this release. 100,000 lamps.

129
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:07,720
That is the conservative count, the count
drawn from the Westinghouse contract and the

130
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:10,000
documented lamp installation per building.

131
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:12,440
Some accounts say more.

132
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:15,340
The number that matters is not the number,

133
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:17,200
but the simultaneity.

134
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:21,740
And the simultaneity is what the
photographs cannot hold.

135
00:10:22,540 --> 00:10:26,380
20 ,000 people made a sound that was not
applause.

136
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:29,500
Applause is a decision.

137
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:36,840
This was not. The involuntary sound a body
produces when the visual field is overwhelmed

138
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:40,880
faster than the nervous system can process
the new information.

139
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:47,580
The sound came from the chest, from the
place in the chest that responds to things

140
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:49,200
before the mind has caught up.

141
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:54,020
Then it became applause as the minds
caught up.

142
00:10:54,980 --> 00:11:00,360
But for a moment, between the lights
coming on and the hands beginning to move,

143
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:04,280
the crowd made the sound of pure
registration.

144
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:11,220
The court of honor was white, white in a
way that the word cannot quite carry.

145
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:18,640
The plaster, the composite of plaster of
Paris and cement and glycerin and jute

146
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:24,320
fiber, trailed over the steel and wood
frames to produce the neoclassical details,

147
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:29,520
had been finished in calcium sulfate,
which is white in daylight.

148
00:11:30,420 --> 00:11:33,220
Under incandescent illumination at this
scale,

149
00:11:34,020 --> 00:11:37,540
it was not white in the way that a white
wall in a room is white.

150
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:42,480
It was white in the way that the inside of
a cloud is white when you

151
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:49,160
are in it. Omni -directional, sauceless,
the light arriving from every

152
00:11:49,160 --> 00:11:53,660
surface simultaneously because every
surface was generating it.

153
00:11:54,840 --> 00:11:57,640
The lagoon received the light and doubled
it.

154
00:11:59,020 --> 00:12:04,200
The water of the lagoon held the reflection
of the court of honor buildings with a

155
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:09,920
precision that still water has when the
air above it is still and the light is

156
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:12,640
strong enough to clarify every surface.

157
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:17,060
The reflection was a second white city,
inverted,

158
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:19,620
suspended below the real one.

159
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:23,840
The columns and cornices were upside down
in the black water.

160
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:27,220
The dome was a dome in both directions.

161
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:34,360
20 ,000 people were standing between the
white city and its mirror image and the light

162
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:37,500
was hitting them from above and below
simultaneously.

163
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:43,720
The sensation of standing in front of
something that looks as if it has been there

164
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:48,440
for a hundred years. This is the part that
requires attention.

165
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:55,400
The buildings were temporary. They had been
built in approximately 30 months using a

166
00:12:55,400 --> 00:13:00,900
composite designed to last the six months
of the fair's run and then be demolished.

167
00:13:01,640 --> 00:13:04,460
The construction record is explicit about
this.

168
00:13:05,420 --> 00:13:09,400
Daniel Burnham's correspondence, the
Westinghouse contract,

169
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:14,880
the building permit, all of it consistent
with a temporary exhibition.

170
00:13:15,900 --> 00:13:17,900
The buildings were not designed to last.

171
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:21,140
They were not built to last.

172
00:13:22,120 --> 00:13:26,200
But under the light of 100,000
incandescent lamps,

173
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:28,520
they looked as if they had.

174
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:35,220
The columns cast shadows with the
sharpness of stone columns under noon sun.

175
00:13:35,760 --> 00:13:38,380
The cornices had the depth of carved
limestone,

176
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:42,500
not the suggestion of depth, but depth
itself.

177
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:50,400
The shadow falling into the recess and the
cornice edge catching the light at an angle

178
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:53,900
that made the plaster surface look like
something quarried.

179
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:57,240
The pediments held their weight against
the sky.

180
00:13:58,140 --> 00:14:03,340
The way pediments hold weight when the stone
behind them is the stone they appear to

181
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:05,720
be. Was it the light?

182
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,560
The official explanation is yes.

183
00:14:10,640 --> 00:14:13,740
The White City's aesthetic program
depended on this.

184
00:14:14,980 --> 00:14:17,020
Burnham had designed it to look permanent.

185
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:23,000
The official photographers had been stationed
at angles that maximized the impression

186
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:24,740
of weight and permanence.

187
00:14:30,380 --> 00:14:32,980
The calcium the eternal.

188
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,780
And it performed it well enough that 20
,000 people on the opening night,

189
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:43,160
and 27 million over the course of five
months,

190
00:14:43,860 --> 00:14:48,820
went home carrying the memory of buildings
that looked like they had stood for a century.

191
00:14:49,740 --> 00:14:51,560
The explanation is complete.

192
00:14:52,360 --> 00:14:54,480
It satisfies at the level of logic.

193
00:14:55,200 --> 00:15:00,620
But look at what a woman named Cecilia
Wren wrote in her diary three days after

194
00:15:00,620 --> 00:15:04,500
the opening. A private diary, never
published,

195
00:15:05,300 --> 00:15:11,720
acquired in a lot of Chicago ephemera by the
same collector who would later acquire James

196
00:15:11,720 --> 00:15:16,160
Coburn's field note, held by the same
estate in Kenosha,

197
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:20,620
Wisconsin, the same estate that dispersed
in 1971.

198
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:25,260
Cecilia Wren was a teacher from Evanston,
Illinois.

199
00:15:26,340 --> 00:15:28,180
She had attended the opening ceremony.

200
00:15:29,060 --> 00:15:33,520
She wrote, I have seen paintings of Rome
and of Athens,

201
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:36,400
and I know what those buildings are made
of.

202
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:41,340
And I know that plaster painted white
cannot be the same thing.

203
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:45,560
But I swear to you, standing in that
light,

204
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:47,880
I could not tell the difference.

205
00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:50,540
It was not a painting of Rome.

206
00:15:50,540 --> 00:15:52,300
It was Rome.

207
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:57,780
And I know that cannot be true, and I
cannot explain why my hands,

208
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:01,240
when I put them on the column near the
central pavilion,

209
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:06,800
felt what they felt. She didn't describe
what her hands felt.

210
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:09,700
She moved on in the diary.

211
00:16:10,780 --> 00:16:13,040
The next paragraph was about dinner.

212
00:16:14,140 --> 00:16:16,780
Cecilia Wren was 51 years old.

213
00:16:17,520 --> 00:16:20,780
She'd grown up in a house with limestone
foundation walls.

214
00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:25,160
She had spent 30 years touching chalk,
slate,

215
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:28,740
and the plaster ceilings of school rooms
in Evanston.

216
00:16:29,860 --> 00:16:32,060
Her hands knew the difference between
things.

217
00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:36,140
She went to the central pavilion on the
third day of the fair.

218
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:40,120
This is in the diary, a Tuesday afternoon.

219
00:16:41,440 --> 00:16:44,140
The crowds thinner on weekday afternoons.

220
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:46,940
The lake wend off the water.

221
00:16:47,980 --> 00:16:53,300
She walked to the nearest column and put
her palm flat against the surface.

222
00:16:54,020 --> 00:16:55,580
The calcium sulfate was there.

223
00:16:56,720 --> 00:17:03,220
She could feel it, the slight grit, the
fine crystalline texture at the very edge of

224
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:09,380
fingertip sensitivity, the specific dry quality
of plaster that has been finished smooth

225
00:17:09,380 --> 00:17:12,460
but not polished. That was the outer
layer.

226
00:17:13,380 --> 00:17:15,020
That was what she expected.

227
00:17:16,080 --> 00:17:19,720
She had run her hands over plaster her
entire life,

228
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:23,760
and she knew what plaster returned against
a bare palm.

229
00:17:24,880 --> 00:17:29,000
But the column was not returning what
plaster returns.

230
00:17:29,900 --> 00:17:32,580
The first thing she noticed was the
temperature.

231
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:37,640
Plaster in May, standing in the open air
for three days,

232
00:17:38,280 --> 00:17:42,360
draws warmth from the sun and releases it
slowly but evenly.

233
00:17:43,420 --> 00:17:46,380
The surface should have been warm in the
afternoon light.

234
00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:50,260
The warmth distributed across the face of
the column,

235
00:17:50,840 --> 00:17:56,560
the way warmth distributes in any porous
composite that absorbs solar radiation at its

236
00:17:56,560 --> 00:17:58,860
surface and doesn't conduct it far.

237
00:17:59,960 --> 00:18:02,600
This column was warmer at the base than
the face.

238
00:18:03,780 --> 00:18:09,720
Warmer in the interior register, not the
surface warmth of something heated from outside,

239
00:18:10,300 --> 00:18:14,640
but the deeper warmth of something that
generates or retains heat from within.

240
00:18:15,900 --> 00:18:22,160
Stone does this. Limestone three feet thick
holds its temperature differently than three

241
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:24,480
inches of plaster over wire length.

242
00:18:25,700 --> 00:18:27,940
The second thing was the return.

243
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:30,940
When her palm pressed against the surface,

244
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:36,080
the surface pressed back. Not hard, not
with any force.

245
00:18:37,020 --> 00:18:40,240
The specific resistance of a thing that
has mass behind it.

246
00:18:41,220 --> 00:18:46,680
Dense mass. Her hand against plaster over
wood framing gets a vibration.

247
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:53,520
A slight give. The sensation of thickness
backed by air and structural members.

248
00:18:54,340 --> 00:18:57,000
Her hand against this column got stone.

249
00:18:57,960 --> 00:19:03,700
The pressure traveled back through her palm and
into her wrist with the quality of something

250
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:09,600
continuous, something that went down through
the floor of the pavilion and kept going.

251
00:19:10,520 --> 00:19:17,420
She pressed harder. The column absorbed
the pressure and returned it unchanged,

252
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:23,060
like pressing your palm against the side
of a building instead of a partition.

253
00:19:24,100 --> 00:19:27,980
She stood there for a moment, then she
moved on to dinner.

254
00:19:28,920 --> 00:19:31,520
She did not tell anyone what she had felt.

255
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:36,280
There was no frame for it. The column was
plaster over wood.

256
00:19:37,260 --> 00:19:40,280
Everyone said so. The signs said so.

257
00:19:41,320 --> 00:19:42,760
The official program said so.

258
00:19:43,940 --> 00:19:49,880
Her hands said something else and her hands
were 51 years old and knew things that

259
00:19:49,880 --> 00:19:51,540
were hard to explain in a diary.

260
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:58,840
So she wrote the sentence and stopped and
went to find her sister at the refreshment

261
00:19:58,840 --> 00:20:01,140
stand near the agriculture building.

262
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:04,700
The next paragraph was about dinner.

263
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:11,100
The photographs are the natural place to
look and the record looked at them first.

264
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:18,120
Thousands of photographs survive from the
1893 World's Columbian Exposition.

265
00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:21,840
Official documentation. Tourist records.

266
00:20:22,980 --> 00:20:25,880
The panorama views taken by the official
photographers.

267
00:20:26,960 --> 00:20:33,140
They are striking images. The scale of the
Court of Honor buildings reads clearly even

268
00:20:33,140 --> 00:20:36,140
in the compressed medium of silver nitrate
