326
00:25:06,400 --> 00:25:09,760
If the construction was being photographed
at multiple stages.

327
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:15,180
Six plates donated by an unnamed estate in
1937.

328
00:25:16,120 --> 00:25:19,440
Six plates that resist the explanations
available.

329
00:25:20,060 --> 00:25:25,260
In a collection that doesn't permit the examination
necessary to fully resolve the question.

330
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:30,180
The photographs were designed to make the
buildings look permanent.

331
00:25:31,380 --> 00:25:34,140
That explanation is complete and accurate.

332
00:25:35,460 --> 00:25:37,620
But now comes James Coburn.

333
00:25:38,880 --> 00:25:44,340
The standard account of how the white city
was built deserves its full weight before

334
00:25:44,340 --> 00:25:48,440
it is destabilized because it is
remarkable on its own terms.

335
00:25:49,300 --> 00:25:55,280
And this account is not served by treating it
as a deception before the evidence requires

336
00:25:55,280 --> 00:25:59,120
that. Staff plaster was an architectural
achievement.

337
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,680
The composite of plaster of Paris, cement,

338
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:04,920
glycerin and binders.

339
00:26:05,860 --> 00:26:08,080
Primarily jute fiber and dextrin.

340
00:26:08,840 --> 00:26:10,460
A starch derived adhesive.

341
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:17,020
Could be cast or troweled over wire lathe
and structural framing to produce detailed

342
00:26:17,020 --> 00:26:22,460
decorative surfaces with a speed that
traditional stone construction couldn't match.

343
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:27,260
European exposition buildings had used
similar techniques for decades.

344
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:34,580
The Paris exposition universe cell of 1889
had demonstrated what temporary exhibition

345
00:26:34,580 --> 00:26:36,200
architecture was capable of.

346
00:26:37,080 --> 00:26:40,140
The Eiffel Tower was the iron anchor of
that fair.

347
00:26:40,840 --> 00:26:46,200
But the surrounding exhibition buildings
were exercises in exactly the kind of rapid

348
00:26:46,200 --> 00:26:49,980
construction grandeur that staff composite
made possible.

349
00:26:50,720 --> 00:26:56,600
What Burnham and his team achieved at Jackson
Park in approximately 30 months was genuine.

350
00:26:57,460 --> 00:27:04,220
The documented construction accounts in
the inland architect from 1892 and 1893

351
00:27:04,220 --> 00:27:06,500
described the pace of the work.

352
00:27:07,600 --> 00:27:09,460
The crews working multiple shifts.

353
00:27:10,400 --> 00:27:15,080
The prefabrication of architectural
details in workshops before installation.

354
00:27:15,980 --> 00:27:22,860
The coordination of dozens of contractors
across 690 acres of former lakefront

355
00:27:22,860 --> 00:27:27,200
swamp and parkland. The compressed
timeline was real.

356
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:33,120
The achievement was real. And the surfaces
were what the record says they were.

357
00:27:34,220 --> 00:27:37,660
Staff plaster was the skin, not the
skeleton.

358
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:41,980
The load bearing elements were the steel
and wood frames.

359
00:27:42,980 --> 00:27:49,940
The plaster composite was applied over
those frames three to four inches enough

360
00:27:49,940 --> 00:27:51,760
to hold detailed surface treatment.

361
00:27:52,820 --> 00:27:54,920
Not enough to stand on its own.

362
00:27:56,300 --> 00:28:01,160
A wall of plaster without its internal
frame would not be a wall.

363
00:28:01,960 --> 00:28:07,600
It would be a shell. And shells can be
photographed to look like anything.

364
00:28:08,560 --> 00:28:11,360
The white city name was Burnham's choice.

365
00:28:12,300 --> 00:28:18,360
The calcium sulfate surface of the staff
plaster read as white in almost any lighting

366
00:28:18,360 --> 00:28:24,680
condition and under the incandescent
illumination of the Westinghouse Tesla system it

367
00:28:24,680 --> 00:28:31,240
read as monumental as the marble of a Rome
or an Athens transported to the lakefront

368
00:28:31,240 --> 00:28:33,660
of Chicago in 1893.

369
00:28:34,760 --> 00:28:37,720
The deception was not a flaw in the
project.

370
00:28:38,480 --> 00:28:44,780
It was the project. The white city was
designed to produce the sensation of permanence

371
00:28:44,780 --> 00:28:50,140
in people who knew at some level that what
they were standing in front of was

372
00:28:50,140 --> 00:28:55,120
temporary. The satisfaction was in the
performance not the substance.

373
00:28:55,980 --> 00:29:03,660
This is the explanation and it is
sufficient and it is true and

374
00:29:03,660 --> 00:29:10,160
it accounts for 27 million people going home
carrying the memory of buildings that looked

375
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:12,540
like stone when they were not stone.

376
00:29:13,460 --> 00:29:19,400
It accounts for everything about the white
city except what James Coburn found inside

377
00:29:19,400 --> 00:29:23,340
the wall. James Coburn was from Gary,
Indiana.

378
00:29:24,460 --> 00:29:29,720
This is in the letter where he gives his
address in the heading the practical information

379
00:29:29,720 --> 00:29:34,380
a man includes when writing to his foreman
in case a reply is needed.

380
00:29:35,080 --> 00:29:49,380
He was hired by a wrecking contractor for
the post fair demolition

381
00:29:49,380 --> 00:29:56,120
of the 1893. Coburn worked on the
manufacturers and liberal arts building between

382
00:29:56,120 --> 00:30:00,060
December of 1893 and April of 1894.

383
00:30:01,180 --> 00:30:06,080
He kept field notes not a formal journal
but a laborer's working record,

384
00:30:06,900 --> 00:30:11,340
daily assignments, surfaces encountered,
problems noted.

385
00:30:12,400 --> 00:30:16,180
The kind of record a careful worker keeps
in case of dispute.

386
00:30:17,340 --> 00:30:19,600
The field notes survive as photocopies.

387
00:30:20,700 --> 00:30:25,620
The originals pass through two subsequent
hands before arriving in a private collection

388
00:30:25,620 --> 00:30:32,540
in Kenosha, Wisconsin, around 1950, part
of a larger lot of Chicago ephemera

389
00:30:32,540 --> 00:30:37,920
at an estate sale. When that estate
dispersed at auction in 1971,

390
00:30:38,820 --> 00:30:44,120
a researcher arranged to have photocopies made
of two of the three items before dispersal.

391
00:30:44,960 --> 00:30:46,700
The researcher didn't publish.

392
00:30:47,760 --> 00:30:50,700
The originals current location hasn't been
established.

393
00:30:51,480 --> 00:30:53,260
The photocopies are what survive.

394
00:30:54,340 --> 00:31:00,080
And what survives in the photocopies across
the daily entries of a careful working man

395
00:31:00,080 --> 00:31:05,840
is a picture of ordinary demolition work
with one extraordinary exception.

396
00:31:07,020 --> 00:31:10,940
The ordinary work reads like what
demolition work reads like,

397
00:31:10,940 --> 00:31:14,300
arrival at the site, assignment to a
section,

398
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:17,840
the physical work of opening walls and
removing debris,

399
00:31:18,740 --> 00:31:21,640
note of what was encountered and how long
it took.

400
00:31:22,740 --> 00:31:29,280
The language is terse. Working notes written
at the end of a long physical day have

401
00:31:29,280 --> 00:31:31,840
a brevity that is not literary choice.

402
00:31:32,720 --> 00:31:35,420
It is exhaustion editing the unnecessary.

403
00:31:36,600 --> 00:31:40,520
Then, in the second week of February,
1894,

404
00:31:42,140 --> 00:31:45,380
had trouble today with south section near
the piers.

405
00:31:46,640 --> 00:31:52,020
Winter, 1894, the Manufactures and Liberal
Arts building.

406
00:31:53,040 --> 00:31:58,380
South Wing, the fair has been over for
three months and the largest building in the

407
00:31:58,380 --> 00:32:00,100
world is being unmade.

408
00:32:01,080 --> 00:32:06,380
James co -burns hands on the wrecking bar
at seven in the morning when the temperature

409
00:32:06,380 --> 00:32:11,740
on the lakefront was well below freezing
and the iron of the bar was cold enough

410
00:32:11,740 --> 00:32:13,480
to feel through work gloves.

411
00:32:14,700 --> 00:32:20,380
Not the comfortable cold of cold handled
tools that have been used for an hour and

412
00:32:20,380 --> 00:32:22,220
have taken some warmth from the hands.

413
00:32:22,980 --> 00:32:29,580
The morning cold, the cold of iron that
has been left outside overnight and has

414
00:32:29,580 --> 00:32:34,500
given up every calorie of retained heat to
the Chicago winter air.

415
00:32:35,500 --> 00:32:38,220
Through the leather and the wool lining of
the gloves,

416
00:32:39,180 --> 00:32:43,760
the cold of the bar reached the palms as a
pressure more than a temperature.

417
00:32:44,740 --> 00:32:50,860
The dense, ungiving cold that reads in the
hands as weight even when the weight hasn't

418
00:32:50,860 --> 00:32:55,420
changed. The section of the building he
was working was the South Wing.

419
00:32:56,380 --> 00:32:59,600
The roof section above this area had been
removed already.

420
00:33:00,600 --> 00:33:03,860
The demolition moved from top down
systematically.

421
00:33:05,220 --> 00:33:09,420
The steel trusses taken first, then the
upper wall sections,

422
00:33:10,460 --> 00:33:16,440
then the interior elements, leaving the exterior
wall of the South Wing as the last major

423
00:33:16,440 --> 00:33:19,760
standing section. Above where Coburn
stood,

424
00:33:20,580 --> 00:33:24,840
the sky was visible, the flat gray of a
Chicago winter morning.

425
00:33:25,620 --> 00:33:28,960
The sky, the color of the inside of a zinc
bucket,

426
00:33:30,020 --> 00:33:35,500
directionless light coming from all of it
equally and providing illumination without

427
00:33:35,500 --> 00:33:39,320
warmth. Other crews were working beyond
the wall.

428
00:33:40,220 --> 00:33:43,960
The sound of it reached him as percussion
through the stone and plaster.

429
00:33:44,940 --> 00:33:48,320
Not loud, filtered by the wall section
between them,

430
00:33:49,220 --> 00:33:53,760
but unmistakable. The heavy thud of mauls
on masonry.

431
00:33:54,500 --> 00:33:57,600
The specific crack of wooden members
giving way.

432
00:33:58,720 --> 00:34:03,620
The occasional pause that meant a man was
repositioning before the next swing.

433
00:34:04,680 --> 00:34:06,300
The sounds of reduction.

434
00:34:07,580 --> 00:34:12,460
The sounds of a building being converted
from a thing that was into a pile that

435
00:34:12,460 --> 00:34:17,340
could be hauled away. The smell in the
opened wall was mineral.

436
00:34:18,560 --> 00:34:21,220
Plaster dust has a smell that is not
exactly a smell.

437
00:34:22,160 --> 00:34:23,920
It is more of a quality of the air.

438
00:34:25,260 --> 00:34:30,660
A dryness that arrives when the calcium sulfate
is broken open and the interior is exposed.

439
00:34:31,680 --> 00:34:37,460
Not unpleasant. The specific dry mineral
quality of a composite that has been sealed

440
00:34:37,460 --> 00:34:40,680
away from moisture and now encounters the
air again.

441
00:34:42,020 --> 00:34:45,000
And underneath that, the ghost of
occupation.

442
00:34:46,220 --> 00:34:50,560
The six months of the fair. The 27 million
visitors.

443
00:34:51,920 --> 00:34:55,920
The cooking from the concessions areas
carried into the building's fabric.

444
00:34:57,040 --> 00:34:59,720
The chemical exhibits in the interior
halls.

445
00:35:00,740 --> 00:35:04,820
All of it absorbed into the pores of the
wall during the fair's run.

446
00:35:04,820 --> 00:35:09,460
And now slowly releasing as the wall was
opened.

447
00:35:10,500 --> 00:35:12,760
Coburn had been doing this for weeks.

448
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:16,960
This is the thing to understand about what
happened next.

449
00:35:17,680 --> 00:35:21,220
The body, after weeks of the same physical
operation,

450
00:35:22,040 --> 00:35:25,580
builds a knowledge that is not conscious
and not articulate.

451
00:35:26,380 --> 00:35:29,940
Not expertise. Nothing so formal as that.

452
00:35:30,860 --> 00:35:33,440
The body's version of expertise is
simpler.

453
00:35:34,360 --> 00:35:40,320
It builds a file. Every time a wrecking bar
connects with the same type of construction,

454
00:35:40,900 --> 00:35:45,980
the vibration that travels up the bar through
the gloves into the wrists and forearms,

455
00:35:46,280 --> 00:35:49,360
and up through the shoulders gets added to
the file.

456
00:35:50,080 --> 00:35:52,580
The nervous system is a recording
instrument.

457
00:35:53,380 --> 00:35:59,300
And after weeks of the same surfaces, the
file is complete enough that any deviation

458
00:35:59,300 --> 00:36:03,780
from it is registered before the mind has
finished processing the impact.

459
00:36:04,540 --> 00:36:07,340
Coburn brought the bar to the section of
wall.

460
00:36:08,220 --> 00:36:13,520
He made contact. The bar didn't respond
the way it had been responding for weeks.

461
00:36:14,700 --> 00:36:17,960
Not wrong in the way that an unexpected
obstruction is wrong.

462
00:36:18,540 --> 00:36:21,120
Not harder, exactly. Not blocked.

463
00:36:21,900 --> 00:36:27,040
Not the specific resistance of a structural
element encountered when the surface should

464
00:36:27,040 --> 00:36:31,060
have been clear. Different in a more
fundamental way.

465
00:36:32,100 --> 00:36:37,120
The vibration that travelled up the iron
and through the gloves and into the bones of

466
00:36:37,120 --> 00:36:41,780
his wrists was not the vibration he had
been accumulating in his file for weeks.

467
00:36:42,800 --> 00:36:44,600
The file said one thing.

468
00:36:45,400 --> 00:36:48,820
The wall said another. He did it again.

469
00:36:49,740 --> 00:36:51,320
The same return.

470
00:36:52,340 --> 00:36:55,540
More complex than plaster over wire lath.

471
00:36:56,260 --> 00:37:01,200
The vibration was carrying more information
than the plaster should have been capable

472
00:37:01,200 --> 00:37:05,880
of carrying. Not just the energy of the
impact returning through the bar,

473
00:37:06,400 --> 00:37:10,000
but something that built after the initial
contact and sustained.

474
00:37:11,180 --> 00:37:14,480
The way a struck bell carries more than
the energy of the striker.

475
00:37:15,160 --> 00:37:17,980
The way the stone converts impact to
vibration.

476
00:37:18,840 --> 00:37:24,060
And how much of that vibration travels
back through the tool into the hand of the

477
00:37:24,060 --> 00:37:30,380
person holding it. His hands were receiving
something that didn't match three weeks of

478
00:37:30,380 --> 00:37:32,440
demolition work on the same building.

479
00:37:33,240 --> 00:37:34,960
He took off the right glove.

480
00:37:35,540 --> 00:37:39,000
He placed his palm flat against the
surface of the wall.

481
00:37:40,200 --> 00:37:41,940
The plaster finish was there.

482
00:37:42,760 --> 00:37:46,760
The slight grit of the calcium sulfate
surface against bare skin.

483
00:37:48,120 --> 00:37:53,000
The crystalline texture, just perceptible
at the very edge of fingertip sensitivity.

484
00:37:54,420 --> 00:37:56,220
This was what he expected.

485
00:37:57,060 --> 00:37:59,780
This was the outer layer of staff
composite.

486
00:38:00,320 --> 00:38:02,780
And it felt exactly like staff composite.

487
00:38:03,460 --> 00:38:05,180
But the temperature was wrong.

488
00:38:06,100 --> 00:38:09,880
The wall had been losing heat to the
Chicago winter for months.

489
00:38:10,680 --> 00:38:12,180
The building was not heated.

490
00:38:13,020 --> 00:38:19,400
The fair was over. The enormous stone faced
rooms were open to the winter through the

491
00:38:19,400 --> 00:38:21,340
sections already demolished above.

492
00:38:22,540 --> 00:38:25,780
A wall in those conditions should be cold
at the core.

493
00:38:26,720 --> 00:38:30,360
The surface should be very nearly the
temperature of the air around it.

494
00:38:30,980 --> 00:38:35,680
The plaster should be cold to the touch
and stay cold against the bare hand.

495
00:38:36,700 --> 00:38:38,960
This section was not cold.

496
00:38:39,540 --> 00:38:43,640
Not warm either. Not the warmth of a
heated space.

497
00:38:44,740 --> 00:38:47,260
Something between cold and room
temperature,

498
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:52,620
closer to body temperature than a wall in
an unheated building in February in Chicago,

499
00:38:53,460 --> 00:39:00,140
had any right to be. As if the limestone
behind the plaster had been losing heat

500
00:39:00,140 --> 00:39:01,680
more slowly than it should.

501
00:39:03,160 --> 00:39:05,420
As if there was something denser in there.

502
00:39:06,760 --> 00:39:11,440
Something that had absorbed the warmth of
the six months of the fair's occupation.

503
00:39:12,500 --> 00:39:15,140
And hadn't yet fully surrendered it to the
winter.

504
00:39:15,860 --> 00:39:19,660
Dense stone retains heat differently than
porous plaster.

505
00:39:20,280 --> 00:39:25,500
Dense limestone can hold what it has
absorbed for a long time after the source of

506
00:39:25,500 --> 00:39:27,100
the absorption has been removed.

507
00:39:27,900 --> 00:39:31,040
His palm against the wall felt the texture
of the plaster.

508
00:39:32,100 --> 00:39:36,980
But through the plaster, beneath it, there
was a different register.

509
00:39:37,940 --> 00:39:44,480
Not hollow. Not the slight give of a
surface that is three or four inches of

510
00:39:44,480 --> 00:39:47,320
composite over wire length and framing.

511
00:39:48,300 --> 00:39:51,380
Solid in a way that went deeper than the
surface.

512
00:39:52,440 --> 00:39:57,020
The kind of solid that doesn't transmit
vibration the way surface plaster does.

513
00:39:57,940 --> 00:39:59,600
That absorbs the strike differently.

514
00:40:00,460 --> 00:40:02,840
That returns more of the energy than it
keeps.

515
00:40:03,620 --> 00:40:05,440
That rings rather than thuds.

516
00:40:06,520 --> 00:40:09,580
He stood there with his bare hand on the
wall.

517
00:40:10,560 --> 00:40:14,920
He stood there long enough for the temperature
of the wall to settle into his palm.

518
00:40:15,900 --> 00:40:18,880
For the palm to stop registering its own
warmth.

519
00:40:19,580 --> 00:40:25,960
As the warmth of the wall. For the distinction
between his skin and the surface to

520
00:40:25,960 --> 00:40:30,400
clarify. He was reading the wall the way a
doctor reads something.

521
00:40:31,120 --> 00:40:33,960
Not examining. Not testing.

522
00:40:34,880 --> 00:40:37,880
But receiving. Letting it tell him.

523
00:40:38,740 --> 00:40:40,620
Then he put his glove back on.

524
00:40:41,920 --> 00:40:44,300
Then he moved to a different section.

525
00:40:45,040 --> 00:40:48,780
Then, at the end of the workday, in his
field notes,

526
00:40:49,180 --> 00:40:52,860
he wrote, had trouble today with south
section near the pier.

527
00:40:53,900 --> 00:40:55,560
Harder than expected in places.

528
00:40:56,700 --> 00:40:58,220
Will need to note for billing.

529
00:40:59,220 --> 00:41:04,760
And then, in the letter to his foreman,
which came several weeks later.

530
00:41:06,140 --> 00:41:10,600
After the trouble had become not an
isolated incident but a pattern.

531
00:41:12,060 --> 00:41:14,200
It gave back more than it should have.

532
00:41:15,020 --> 00:41:17,020
Like ringing a pan and hearing a bell.

533
00:41:18,300 --> 00:41:21,020
Some sections I could not work the
standard way.

534
00:41:22,140 --> 00:41:26,640
In the south colonnade section, near the
base of the second pier from the east,

535
00:41:27,220 --> 00:41:29,140
there was stone there. Mr.

536
00:41:29,320 --> 00:41:31,060
H. Not staff.

537
00:41:32,180 --> 00:41:34,360
I do not know how to account for it.

538
00:41:34,920 --> 00:41:37,040
The stone ran below the floor level.

539
00:41:38,020 --> 00:41:40,800
I did not dig to find where it ended.

540
00:41:42,200 --> 00:41:47,540
He was not writing a mystery. He was
writing documentation for a billing dispute.

541
00:41:48,740 --> 00:41:54,540
He was a working man, noting a practical
problem in the only language his position gave

542
00:41:54,540 --> 00:41:57,560
him. The language happened to be precise.

543
00:41:59,020 --> 00:42:04,360
Working men whose daily occupation is the
physical differentiation of surfaces develop

544
00:42:04,360 --> 00:42:08,440
a precision about construction that is not
literary and not theoretical.

545
00:42:09,500 --> 00:42:13,700
It is functional. When James Coburn wrote
the word stone,

546
00:42:14,560 --> 00:42:18,820
he meant stone. He had been working with
plaster for weeks.

547
00:42:19,660 --> 00:42:25,260
He knew the difference. The narrator does
not know what happened next.

548
00:42:26,980 --> 00:42:29,960
Coburn's field notes continue after this
date,

549
00:42:30,620 --> 00:42:33,400
but they move to a different section of
the building.

550
00:42:34,400 --> 00:42:39,540
The south colonnade section near the second
pier from the east doesn't appear in the

551
00:42:39,540 --> 00:42:42,240
subsequent entries. He moved on.

552
00:42:43,420 --> 00:42:49,080
He didn't go back. Whether his foreman
responded to the letter is not established.

553
00:42:49,680 --> 00:42:52,160
There is no reply in the surviving record.

554
00:42:53,100 --> 00:42:58,480
Whether the billing dispute that prompted the
documentation was resolved is not established.

555
00:42:59,480 --> 00:43:04,100
And then, in the final week of April,
1894,

556
00:43:05,100 --> 00:43:11,620
the field notes end. They end at a point
where Coburn had noted his intention to

557
00:43:11,620 --> 00:43:13,860
move to the northwest section of the
building,

558
00:43:14,740 --> 00:43:16,720
the section he hadn't yet reached.

559
00:43:17,620 --> 00:43:21,260
The final entry describes his assignment
for the following day.

560
00:43:22,240 --> 00:43:23,640
Northwest section.

561
00:43:24,400 --> 00:43:28,720
Ground level. There is no entry for the
following day.

562
00:43:29,700 --> 00:43:34,640
The notebook ends. The page after the
final entry is blank.

563
00:43:35,560 --> 00:43:38,120
The follow -up letter he had mentioned
writing.

564
00:43:39,120 --> 00:43:44,380
The one that would describe what he found in
the northwest section doesn't exist in the

565
00:43:44,380 --> 00:43:48,700
surviving record. The collection dispersed
in 1971.

566
00:43:48,700 --> 00:43:53,000
The original's current location hasn't
been established.

567
00:43:53,940 --> 00:44:00,280
There is no record of James Coburn in any
Chicago city directory after 1894.

568
00:44:01,500 --> 00:44:07,100
No death certificate bearing his name has
been located in the Cook County records for

569
00:44:07,100 --> 00:44:13,680
that period. No labor record from any subsequent
contractor project in the Chicago area

570
00:44:13,680 --> 00:44:15,340
has produced his name.

571
00:44:15,340 --> 00:44:18,160
The record notes the absence.

572
00:44:19,140 --> 00:44:23,840
It doesn't fill it. What did he find in
the northwest section?

573
00:44:25,440 --> 00:44:27,720
March 7th, 1894.

574
00:44:28,800 --> 00:44:30,360
Transportation building annex.

575
00:44:31,220 --> 00:44:37,980
Jackson Park. The City of Chicago Bureau
of Public Works maintained forms for

576
00:44:37,980 --> 00:44:41,740
contractor incidents during demolition and
construction projects.

577
00:44:42,760 --> 00:44:47,340
The forms were standard, date, location,
nature of incident,

578
00:44:48,040 --> 00:44:51,340
names of injured workers, surface
conditions,

579
00:44:52,220 --> 00:44:56,200
supervisors' notation. They were not
designed for mystery.

580
00:44:57,120 --> 00:45:02,560
They were designed for the insurance liability
record and the workers' compensation filings

581
00:45:02,560 --> 00:45:08,220
that an urban government accumulates over decades
of large -scale construction and demolition.

582
00:45:09,240 --> 00:45:13,260
Two workers injured on March 7th, 1894.

583
00:45:14,400 --> 00:45:17,880
The incident report describes the nature
of the incident.

584
00:45:18,860 --> 00:45:22,940
The crew was working a wall section in the
transportation building annex,

585
00:45:23,600 --> 00:45:28,780
grid 14. The wall section proved resistant
to the standard demolition method.

586
00:45:29,640 --> 00:45:31,900
The crew applied additional force.

587
00:45:31,900 --> 00:45:34,780
The material gave suddenly.

588
00:45:35,860 --> 00:45:38,920
Two workers were struck by displaced
sections.

589
00:45:40,080 --> 00:45:44,080
In the field labelled material conditions
contributing to incident,

590
00:45:44,960 --> 00:45:50,100
the superintendent wrote in his own hand,
wall section in grid 14,

591
00:45:50,760 --> 00:45:56,560
unexpectedly resistant, material not
consistent with expected staff construction.

592
00:45:57,920 --> 00:46:00,080
Crew applied additional force.

593
00:46:01,040 --> 00:46:02,580
Material gave suddenly.

594
00:46:03,740 --> 00:46:06,900
Two workers struck by displaced sections.

595
00:46:08,060 --> 00:46:11,760
Material not consistent with expected
staff construction.

596
00:46:12,940 --> 00:46:17,080
The superintendent who wrote this didn't
know James Coburn.

597
00:46:17,860 --> 00:46:19,620
He hadn't read the letter to Mr.

598
00:46:19,780 --> 00:46:26,060
H. He hadn't stood with his bare hand on
the South Colonnade Wall and felt what

599
00:46:26,060 --> 00:46:32,320
Coburn felt. He was filling out a mandatory
form after two members of his crew had

600
00:46:32,320 --> 00:46:38,680
been hurt and the field that asked him to
describe surface conditions received an accurate

601
00:46:38,680 --> 00:46:41,700
answer. He was not theorising.

602
00:46:42,300 --> 00:46:47,400
He was reporting. This is what two
independent observers,

603
00:46:48,400 --> 00:46:53,920
a demolition labourer working the manufacturers
and liberal arts building and a contractor's

604
00:46:53,920 --> 00:46:57,060
superintendent working the transportation
building annex,

605
00:46:57,920 --> 00:47:02,200
described across two months of demolition
work at the Jackson Park site.

606
00:47:03,140 --> 00:47:06,040
Not the same building, not the same
section,

607
00:47:07,100 --> 00:47:13,840
not the same project and not the same
vocabulary because one was a working

608
00:47:13,840 --> 00:47:20,240
man writing to his foreman and the other
was a supervisor filing a mandatory form.

609
00:47:21,280 --> 00:47:22,800
The vocabulary was different.

610
00:47:23,960 --> 00:47:25,700
The observation was the same.

611
00:47:26,480 --> 00:47:30,140
Material not consistent with expected
staff construction.

612
00:47:31,320 --> 00:47:36,880
The incident report surfaced through a
footnote in an unpublished master's thesis on

613
00:47:36,880 --> 00:47:43,260
Chicago labour history written in 1967 at
the University of Illinois Chicago.

614
00:47:44,240 --> 00:47:47,080
The thesis is accessible at the Richard J.

615
00:47:47,080 --> 00:47:52,420
Daily Library. The source document it
references isn't archived with the thesis.

616
00:47:53,280 --> 00:47:55,760
It sits somewhere in the genuinely
extensive,

617
00:47:56,400 --> 00:48:02,260
genuinely unsorted sections of the Illinois
State Archives Bureau of Public Works Holdings

618
00:48:02,260 --> 00:48:07,120
from the 1890 to 1910 period without a
catalogue number.

619
00:48:07,960 --> 00:48:10,480
The record does not know what to make of
this.

620
00:48:11,360 --> 00:48:15,180
Two separate demolition operations,
separate buildings,

621
00:48:15,840 --> 00:48:18,920
separate personnel, separate months.

622
00:48:19,980 --> 00:48:22,920
One a laborer's private note about a
billing dispute.

623
00:48:23,560 --> 00:48:28,020
One a supervisor's mandatory incident
report after workers were hurt.

624
00:48:29,100 --> 00:48:32,980
Neither of them was investigating the
White City's construction materials.

625
00:48:33,820 --> 00:48:39,960
Both of them were doing their jobs and
both of them noted in the process of

626
00:48:39,960 --> 00:48:45,340
doing their jobs that the material they
encountered was not the material they expected

627
00:48:45,340 --> 00:48:50,780
to encounter. The documentation that
should exist does not exist.

628
00:48:52,080 --> 00:48:58,520
Standard municipal practice for demolition
projects of this scale in the 1890s included

629
00:48:58,520 --> 00:49:04,000
contractor progress reports, Bureau of
Public Works site clearance documentation,

630
00:49:04,860 --> 00:49:10,980
final disposition records for a project the
size of the Jackson Park Exposition Demolition,

631
00:49:11,960 --> 00:49:15,280
690 acres, dozens of major structures.

632
00:49:16,240 --> 00:49:19,680
The expected volume of documentation would
be substantial.

633
00:49:20,640 --> 00:49:27,240
The Chicago History Museum holds records
from the 1893 World's Columbian Exposition.

634
00:49:28,160 --> 00:49:33,640
The Illinois State Archives holds records from
the Bureau of Public Works from this period.

635
00:49:34,000 --> 00:49:37,960
The demolition documentation for the Court
of Honor Buildings,

636
00:49:38,420 --> 00:49:42,100
the standard contractor filings, the
Bureau progress reports,

637
00:49:42,760 --> 00:49:47,000
the final clearance records, is absent
from both collections,

638
00:49:47,940 --> 00:49:52,860
not incomplete, not partially available,
absent.

639
00:49:59,760 --> 00:50:05,640
What the demolition crews found is not in
the format that would have recorded it,

640
00:50:05,900 --> 00:50:07,580
is not in the archives.

641
00:50:09,160 --> 00:50:12,860
Now consider what that absence means in
physical terms.

642
00:50:14,080 --> 00:50:18,920
In December of 1893, somewhere in the
Court of Honor,

643
00:50:19,800 --> 00:50:25,600
a wrecking crew swung their first maul into
the first finished wall of the Manufacturers

644
00:50:25,600 --> 00:50:31,160
and Liberal Arts Building. The impact would
have been different depending on what the

645
00:50:31,160 --> 00:50:37,980
wall contained. Staff plaster over wire
lath makes a particular sound when struck.

646
00:50:38,740 --> 00:50:43,680
A flat, concussive thud, the energy
absorbed by the composite,

647
00:50:44,500 --> 00:50:47,680
the wall cracking and falling forward in
irregular fragments.

648
00:50:49,160 --> 00:50:53,720
Workers who have done this kind of work
for a week know the sound without thinking

649
00:50:53,720 --> 00:50:59,360
about it. It settles into the body the way
any repeated rhythm settles.

650
00:51:00,320 --> 00:51:03,560
Now imagine the crew whose maul hit
something else.

651
00:51:04,800 --> 00:51:06,520
The sound changes first.

652
00:51:07,300 --> 00:51:13,180
The thud goes deeper, carries longer, comes
back through the handle with more information.

653
00:51:14,320 --> 00:51:15,800
The fragments don't fall.

654
00:51:16,800 --> 00:51:22,080
They hold. The wall resists in a way that
makes the crew look at each other

655
00:51:22,080 --> 00:51:23,860
before looking at the wall.

656
00:51:25,120 --> 00:51:26,840
Then they apply more force.

657
00:51:27,820 --> 00:51:29,260
The stone gives suddenly.

658
00:51:30,420 --> 00:51:36,140
This is what the Bureau of Public Works Incident
Report would later record for a different

659
00:51:36,140 --> 00:51:37,900
building, a different crew.

660
00:51:38,720 --> 00:51:42,680
And the displaced sections are heavy, not
plaster light,

661
00:51:43,440 --> 00:51:47,280
stone heavy. Workers feel that difference
in their feet.

662
00:51:48,040 --> 00:51:51,680
A section of plaster falling three feet
lands with a dull crunch.

663
00:51:52,600 --> 00:51:55,580
The composite breaking along its own
internal weaknesses.

664
00:51:56,720 --> 00:51:59,100
Raising a brief cloud of calcium dust.

665
00:52:00,120 --> 00:52:06,160
A limestone block falling three feet hits
the floor with a crack that travels through

666
00:52:06,160 --> 00:52:11,200
the slab and into the boots of every man
standing within 20 feet of it.

667
00:52:12,060 --> 00:52:15,020
Not a sound. A statement.

668
00:52:16,440 --> 00:52:19,720
The ground speaking upward through the
soles of their shoes.

669
00:52:20,840 --> 00:52:23,420
Telling them what just came down.

670
00:52:24,460 --> 00:52:27,080
That is what the demolition crews found.

671
00:52:27,760 --> 00:52:30,940
And the records of what they found are not
in the archives.

672
00:52:31,820 --> 00:52:37,480
Chicago is a city that has twice participated
in the removal of its own ground floor.

673
00:52:38,320 --> 00:52:40,360
The first time was not the fair.

674
00:52:41,240 --> 00:52:45,040
The first time was the 1850s and 1860s.

675
00:52:45,040 --> 00:52:50,080
When the city raised its street grade by
approximately four to seven feet,

676
00:52:50,600 --> 00:52:53,300
in the areas most affected by the drainage
problem.

677
00:52:54,280 --> 00:52:55,900
The problem was straightforward.

678
00:52:57,120 --> 00:53:01,200
Chicago's original grade was almost
exactly at the level of Lake Michigan,

679
00:53:01,580 --> 00:53:04,540
which meant that water didn't drain away
from the city.

680
00:53:05,440 --> 00:53:08,360
It pooled. It carried cholera.

681
00:53:09,220 --> 00:53:14,840
In 1854, the epidemic killed six percent
of the city's population.

682
00:53:15,940 --> 00:53:18,380
The engineer, Ellis Sylvester Chesborough,

683
00:53:18,960 --> 00:53:20,460
was commissioned to solve it.

684
00:53:21,940 --> 00:53:24,300
The solution was the street grade raise.

685
00:53:25,620 --> 00:53:32,280
Phil was deposited under the street, raising
the road surface by four to seven feet in

686
00:53:32,280 --> 00:53:36,500
the central district and tapering to
nothing at the perimeter.

687
00:53:37,560 --> 00:53:40,760
It was an engineering achievement of the
first order,

688
00:53:41,480 --> 00:53:43,640
and it was accomplished building by
building,

689
00:53:44,220 --> 00:53:48,000
block by block, over the course of more
than a decade.

690
00:53:49,240 --> 00:53:51,360
Buildings were raised on jack screws.

691
00:53:52,380 --> 00:53:57,740
Entire structures lifted in tact while the
fill was deposited beneath them.

692
00:53:58,780 --> 00:54:03,960
The Briggs House Hotel was famously
elevated while guests were inside,

693
00:54:04,760 --> 00:54:09,120
