920
01:12:04,860 --> 01:12:06,880
system running through structures.

921
01:12:08,100 --> 01:12:11,940
The field moved through whatever the walls
were made of for five months.

922
01:12:13,160 --> 01:12:16,980
Different stones respond differently to a
rotating magnetic field.

923
01:12:17,780 --> 01:12:19,760
The response is a function of
conductivity,

924
01:12:20,640 --> 01:12:22,700
density, crystalline structure.

925
01:12:23,920 --> 01:12:28,780
Staff plaster, calcium sulfate, glycerin,
jute fiber,

926
01:12:29,480 --> 01:12:35,420
responds negligibly. Its molecular structure
doesn't have the ordered geometry that produces

927
01:12:35,420 --> 01:12:37,600
significant electromagnetic interaction.

928
01:12:38,480 --> 01:12:43,500
It absorbs the field passively, like a
sponge absorbs water without being changed by

929
01:12:43,500 --> 01:12:46,580
it. Cut limestone is not plaster.

930
01:12:46,580 --> 01:12:52,440
Limestone is calcium carbonate, a
different crystalline structure,

931
01:12:53,220 --> 01:12:59,100
a different density, a different relationship
between the stone's geometry and an external

932
01:12:59,100 --> 01:13:05,860
field. Tesla knew from the Colorado
Springs experiment and from the theoretical

933
01:13:05,860 --> 01:13:09,820
foundation of his transmitter work that
the earth itself,

934
01:13:10,720 --> 01:13:13,600
fundamentally a limestone and mineral
aggregate,

935
01:13:13,600 --> 01:13:18,940
responded to properly induced frequencies
in ways that plaster did not.

936
01:13:19,840 --> 01:13:23,440
If the South Colonnade Pier contained cut
limestone,

937
01:13:23,980 --> 01:13:27,040
where the specifications said it should
contain plaster,

938
01:13:28,460 --> 01:13:30,600
what was Tesla's current running through?

939
01:13:31,420 --> 01:13:34,520
What was the rotating field interacting
with?

940
01:13:35,460 --> 01:13:38,060
The investigation does not know.

941
01:13:38,780 --> 01:13:42,240
The investigation has three documents and
a question.

942
01:13:43,080 --> 01:13:47,680
One building survives. The Palace of Fine
Arts,

943
01:13:48,200 --> 01:13:52,320
the building that housed the world's art
collection for the duration of the fair,

944
01:13:52,960 --> 01:13:55,880
was built to a different specification
than the rest.

945
01:13:56,900 --> 01:14:01,880
Its primary structure was brick, intended
from the beginning to be more durable,

946
01:14:02,640 --> 01:14:07,980
intended to house works whose loss would have
been permanent rather than merely inconvenient.

947
01:14:09,040 --> 01:14:14,300
The staff exterior was still applied over
the brick and the building still read as a

948
01:14:14,300 --> 01:14:17,420
white city building in the fair's
aesthetic program.

949
01:14:18,340 --> 01:14:22,800
After the fair closed, the Palace of Fine
Arts was not immediately demolished.

950
01:14:23,680 --> 01:14:26,820
It stood. It degraded slowly.

951
01:14:27,760 --> 01:14:29,420
The plaster exterior crumbling.

952
01:14:30,400 --> 01:14:35,420
The brick underneath revealing itself in
sections where the surface had failed.

953
01:14:36,340 --> 01:14:40,340
By the 1920s, the building that survived
was a ruin.

954
01:14:41,340 --> 01:14:43,840
Its plaster skin gone in many places.

955
01:14:44,640 --> 01:14:46,700
Its brick core intact but exposed.

956
01:14:47,920 --> 01:14:51,580
The neoclassical proportions preserved but
stripped of their surface.

957
01:14:52,380 --> 01:14:57,320
Beginning in the late 1920s, the building
was rebuilt in Indiana limestone.

958
01:14:58,100 --> 01:15:00,360
Not restored, rebuilt.

959
01:15:01,740 --> 01:15:05,340
Workers quarried and laid real stone over
the brick core,

960
01:15:05,960 --> 01:15:09,440
replacing the plaster exterior with
permanent construction.

961
01:15:10,420 --> 01:15:13,540
The kind that would survive, not the
duration of a fair,

962
01:15:13,920 --> 01:15:15,880
but the duration of a century.

963
01:15:16,980 --> 01:15:23,740
The building opened in its current form in
1933 as the Museum of Science and Industry.

964
01:15:24,720 --> 01:15:26,380
The limestone is real.

965
01:15:27,200 --> 01:15:32,580
It was laid by workers in the late 1920s
who built over what had been there

966
01:15:32,580 --> 01:15:38,500
before. The Museum of Science and Industry
stands at Jackson Park today.

967
01:15:39,600 --> 01:15:42,860
It is built on the footprint of the Palace
of Fine Arts.

968
01:15:43,580 --> 01:15:47,860
Below it, below the limestone, and below
the brick,

969
01:15:48,900 --> 01:15:50,800
and below the original foundation.

970
01:15:51,860 --> 01:15:57,380
What Alcott found in the South Colonnade Zone
of the Manufactures and Liberal Arts Building

971
01:15:57,380 --> 01:15:59,440
footprint is still there.

972
01:16:00,220 --> 01:16:03,100
Not the Museum. Not the limestone.

973
01:16:04,020 --> 01:16:09,660
Not the brick. What was below grade in
1904 hasn't been excavated.

974
01:16:10,440 --> 01:16:14,160
What Alcott described is still in the soil
of Jackson Park,

975
01:16:14,720 --> 01:16:18,160
at the depth consistent with Foundation
Peer Extension,

976
01:16:18,600 --> 01:16:21,040
exactly where Coburn found it from.

977
01:16:21,840 --> 01:16:25,440
Above, 11 years before Alcott found it
from below.

978
01:16:26,620 --> 01:16:29,220
The demolition reached the above grade
structure.

979
01:16:30,340 --> 01:16:32,100
It didn't reach what was below.

980
01:16:32,880 --> 01:16:36,580
The things that remain are the things the
demolition missed.

981
01:16:37,280 --> 01:16:41,800
The Museum of Science and Industry at
Jackson Park is worth visiting.

982
01:16:42,600 --> 01:16:46,040
Not for the exhibits. The exhibits are
very good,

983
01:16:46,760 --> 01:16:49,660
but they are not why the investigation
mentions it.

984
01:16:50,600 --> 01:16:56,020
The Museum is worth visiting, because when
you stand on its south side and look at

985
01:16:56,020 --> 01:17:00,780
the foundation, not the façade, not the
limestone facing,

986
01:17:01,700 --> 01:17:05,700
but the lowest visible course of stone
where the building meets the grade.

987
01:17:06,600 --> 01:17:12,560
You are looking at Indiana limestone,
quarried and laid in the late 1920s.

988
01:17:13,300 --> 01:17:17,500
Above brick, fired and laid in the early
1890s.

989
01:17:17,960 --> 01:17:23,440
Above whatever the original foundation
contains at depth in the soil of Jackson Park.

990
01:17:24,420 --> 01:17:31,480
At the location where a structural engineer
in 1904 positioned his boring equipment and

991
01:17:31,480 --> 01:17:35,980
recorded what it encountered. He was
surprised by what he found.

992
01:17:36,960 --> 01:17:41,300
He was a professional. He had been taking
soil borings for years.

993
01:17:42,000 --> 01:17:43,720
He wasn't surprised by soil.

994
01:17:44,500 --> 01:17:47,100
He wrote it down and moved to the next
boring.

995
01:17:48,020 --> 01:17:52,640
What surprises an engineer doing routine
fieldwork is not dramatic.

996
01:17:53,560 --> 01:17:57,580
What surprises an engineer doing routine
fieldwork is a number,

997
01:17:58,660 --> 01:18:01,520
a resistance measurement, a density
reading,

998
01:18:02,380 --> 01:18:07,620
a stone composition that doesn't match what
the project specification said should be

999
01:18:07,620 --> 01:18:14,840
there, not a revelation, a discrepancy, a
discrepancy that requires documentation

1000
01:18:14,840 --> 01:18:18,760
because a professional cannot simply skip
past a discrepancy.

1001
01:18:19,580 --> 01:18:26,300
He wrote, density and composition more
consistent with cut limestone aggregate

1002
01:18:26,300 --> 01:18:28,440
than with mixed cement staff.

1003
01:18:29,680 --> 01:18:32,100
Then he moved to the next boring.

1004
01:18:33,520 --> 01:18:38,500
The narrator has been watching him do this
for a while and what cannot be shaken

1005
01:18:38,500 --> 01:18:42,540
is the specificity of the phrase, not
unusual,

1006
01:18:43,240 --> 01:18:47,980
not anomalous, more consistent with cut
limestone aggregate.

1007
01:18:48,780 --> 01:18:53,520
The language of a man who examined the core
sample and recognized what he was looking

1008
01:18:53,520 --> 01:18:56,000
at and wrote down what he recognized.

1009
01:18:57,060 --> 01:19:00,760
Cut limestone aggregate, not geological
limestone,

1010
01:19:01,540 --> 01:19:04,480
not the native bedrock of the Chicago
lakefront area.

1011
01:19:05,360 --> 01:19:08,580
Cut. The word implies quarrying.

1012
01:19:09,600 --> 01:19:13,240
The word implies someone selected this
stone and placed it here,

1013
01:19:14,040 --> 01:19:19,420
placed it below where the south colonnade of
the manufacturers and liberal arts building

1014
01:19:19,420 --> 01:19:24,320
stood, below where James Coburn found it
from above,

1015
01:19:25,140 --> 01:19:31,920
11 years earlier, below where it had been
when Tesla's alternating current ran

1016
01:19:31,920 --> 01:19:37,020
through every wall for six months, below
the demolished building.

1017
01:19:38,220 --> 01:19:42,220
Still there, here is what the
investigation can offer.

1018
01:19:43,500 --> 01:19:47,640
Go outside. If you are near a city, any
city,

1019
01:19:48,460 --> 01:19:52,400
find the oldest building on your street,
not the one that looks oldest,

1020
01:19:53,200 --> 01:19:58,360
the one that is oldest, the one with the
most history between it and the street

1021
01:19:58,360 --> 01:20:02,560
it faces. Walk to it, stand in front of
it,

1022
01:20:03,240 --> 01:20:08,000
then look down, not at the facade, not at
the windows,

1023
01:20:08,680 --> 01:20:12,540
not at the architectural details that were
put there for you to look at.

1024
01:20:13,560 --> 01:20:15,780
Look at where the building enters the
ground,

1025
01:20:16,220 --> 01:20:22,440
the foundation, the base course of stone
or brick that is below the visible face of

1026
01:20:22,440 --> 01:20:27,180
the building, the part that sits in the
earth or below the sidewalk grade.

1027
01:20:28,260 --> 01:20:29,800
Look at what it is made of.

1028
01:20:30,640 --> 01:20:35,020
Feel the surface of it, if you can reach
it without difficulty.

1029
01:20:36,160 --> 01:20:37,760
Put your palm against it.

1030
01:20:38,840 --> 01:20:41,740
Consider that you have been walking past
this building,

1031
01:20:42,240 --> 01:20:46,680
your whole time in this neighborhood, and
you have been seeing the part of it that

1032
01:20:46,680 --> 01:20:51,240
faces the street, the part someone
selected to face the street,

1033
01:20:51,960 --> 01:20:55,480
and the part below grade was never
selected for anyone.

1034
01:20:56,320 --> 01:20:59,100
It was placed there before there was a
street to face.

1035
01:20:59,940 --> 01:21:03,500
It was placed before anyone was thinking
about what would be visible.

1036
01:21:04,860 --> 01:21:06,660
It is what it is without performance.

1037
01:21:07,980 --> 01:21:11,020
Most foundations are exactly what they
appear to be,

1038
01:21:11,860 --> 01:21:17,060
brick or stone or poured concrete, the
straightforward construction of their era,

1039
01:21:18,200 --> 01:21:21,660
the expected answer to the question of
what holds a building in the ground.

1040
01:21:22,640 --> 01:21:29,020
Some are not. Some have stone below grade
that doesn't quite match the stone above.

1041
01:21:30,320 --> 01:21:34,000
Some have foundations that predate the
building they support.

1042
01:21:35,220 --> 01:21:39,740
Some have been sitting below the street
level since before the street was raised,

1043
01:21:40,300 --> 01:21:43,280
since before the neighborhood was built
around them,

1044
01:21:44,380 --> 01:21:47,820
since before the record that would explain
them was written.

1045
01:21:49,000 --> 01:21:50,820
The record accounts for the surface.

1046
01:21:51,960 --> 01:21:54,720
It does not always account for what is
underneath.

1047
01:21:55,720 --> 01:21:58,100
This is not a call to tear things up.

1048
01:21:58,900 --> 01:22:00,880
Alcott didn't tear things up.

1049
01:22:01,620 --> 01:22:06,260
He noted what he found and moved to the
next boring and submitted his report,

1050
01:22:06,700 --> 01:22:09,080
and the development group declined to
proceed,

1051
01:22:09,600 --> 01:22:15,720
and the report went into his professional
archive and from there into a transfer inventory

1052
01:22:15,720 --> 01:22:20,360
listing it as not yet catalogued where it
remains to this day.

1053
01:22:21,480 --> 01:22:24,860
He found it. He recorded it.

1054
01:22:25,700 --> 01:22:31,900
He moved on. The investigation does not
think he was wrong to move on.

1055
01:22:32,720 --> 01:22:36,000
The investigation thinks he was right to
write it down.

1056
01:22:37,360 --> 01:22:41,820
James Coburn wrote it down, not because he
had a theory,

1057
01:22:42,520 --> 01:22:44,740
not because he was looking for an anomaly,

1058
01:22:45,320 --> 01:22:48,340
because he was a working man doing his job
carefully.

1059
01:22:49,260 --> 01:22:54,540
The kind of man who keeps records against
the possibility that a dispute will require

1060
01:22:54,540 --> 01:23:00,780
documentation. The kind of man who puts
his bare hand on a surface to confirm what

1061
01:23:00,780 --> 01:23:02,200
his gloved hands told him.

1062
01:23:03,560 --> 01:23:09,740
The kind of man whose hands had spent enough
time learning one surface that they recognized

1063
01:23:09,740 --> 01:23:12,100
immediately when they met another.

1064
01:23:13,600 --> 01:23:15,840
His hands recognized what they touched.

1065
01:23:17,120 --> 01:23:21,480
He wrote, It gave back more than it should
have,

1066
01:23:22,380 --> 01:23:24,460
like ringing a pan and hearing a bell.

1067
01:23:26,100 --> 01:23:28,660
The letter is in someone's hands right
now,

1068
01:23:29,700 --> 01:23:35,240
or the photocopy of the letter, the durable
version that survived when the original was

1069
01:23:35,240 --> 01:23:40,240
dispersed, and the person holding it is
holding it with both hands.

1070
01:23:41,180 --> 01:23:45,440
Elbows bent. The letter cupped rather than
held.

1071
01:23:46,700 --> 01:23:52,460
The grip of someone who has been surprised
once and cannot afford to be surprised again.

1072
01:23:53,760 --> 01:23:55,600
The fold lines are worn through.

1073
01:23:56,760 --> 01:23:59,580
The paper opens without being told.

1074
01:24:00,460 --> 01:24:07,060
Someone has returned to this and kept
finding it

1075
01:24:07,060 --> 01:24:10,260
necessary to return to it, to read that
passage again,

1076
01:24:11,140 --> 01:24:14,340
to verify once more that the words are
what they appear to be.

1077
01:24:15,180 --> 01:24:18,120
Not a discovery that changes everything.

1078
01:24:18,880 --> 01:24:21,260
Not proof of something hidden.

1079
01:24:22,080 --> 01:24:28,040
Just a careful man's note about a wall that
didn't behave the way the specification said

1080
01:24:28,040 --> 01:24:31,240
it should, in a building that was supposed
to be temporary,

1081
01:24:31,240 --> 01:24:35,720
in a city that was built to perform
permanence and was then removed.

1082
01:24:37,180 --> 01:24:38,880
Almost entirely removed.

1083
01:24:39,920 --> 01:24:43,060
The things that remain are the things the
demolition missed.

1084
01:24:44,340 --> 01:24:46,180
He was going to write down more.

1085
01:24:46,920 --> 01:24:49,780
He was going to document the northwest
section.

1086
01:24:51,020 --> 01:24:53,880
The notebook ends at the page before that
section.

1087
01:24:54,720 --> 01:24:56,920
The follow -up letter doesn't exist.

1088
01:24:57,780 --> 01:25:03,180
The investigation does not know what James
Coburn found in the northwest section.

1089
01:25:04,040 --> 01:25:09,620
The investigation does not know where
James Coburn went after April of 1894.

1090
01:25:10,540 --> 01:25:14,060
The investigation knows what he found
before he stopped writing,

1091
01:25:14,560 --> 01:25:19,200
and what he found is still in the ground
at the South Colonnade Zone of the

1092
01:25:19,200 --> 01:25:23,840
Jackson Park Exposition Footprint at the
depth RT Alcott,

1093
01:25:24,340 --> 01:25:30,900
measured in October of 1904, below the
brick and the limestone and the street

1094
01:25:30,900 --> 01:25:35,380
level world above it. Find the oldest
building on your street.

1095
01:25:36,300 --> 01:25:39,960
Look at the foundation. Look at what it is
made of.

1096
01:25:40,820 --> 01:25:45,300
Put your hand on it. What do you feel
coming back?
